The paper’s main dichotomy does not by itself justify the prior “plant any bounded cut” idea: its NP-completeness is worst-case, while a planted distribution can still be easy. The crucial native hardness route is Theorem 4.3 (numbering in the current arXiv source): ordinary \(d\)-Cut remains NP-complete on bipartite graphs, and any bipartite input becomes a partitioned probe split graph by declaring one side non-probes. I’m checking whether the existing generator genuinely preserves that native problem and whether its declared Track B shortcut has a meaningful mechanical-versus-compact gap.
