Completed a self-contained Track B generator based on the paper’s probe-split \(1\)-Cut construction ([arXiv:2505.22351](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.22351)).

Deliverables:

- [Generator module](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2505.22351/gen_2505_22351.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2505.22351/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2505.22351/README.md)
- [Bare oracle transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2505.22351/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Structural-hint transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2505.22351/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2505.22351/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Key results:

- All G1–G9(c) gates pass.
- Bare hardening: 0/3 solved, verdict `hardened` at `easy`.
- G9: structural hint 0/3; placebo 1/3.
- Structured guessing: 0/200,000.
- Four adversarial heuristics: 0/8 each.
- Reference algorithm: median 23,275 operations, approximately 0.0005 seconds.
- Compact intended route: 228 operations.
- Extended audit: 200/200 valid constructions and reference recoveries; 0/200 successes for each heuristic attack; 200 distinct canonical keys.
- Twenty emitted records were simulated in memory and all were JSON-native and canonically distinct.

Track B is declared honestly because min-cut plus exact GF(2) elimination solves this generated subclass efficiently. I did not run `submit.sh`, since it commits and pushes. Temporary G9 working directories were moved to trash after their transcripts were copied and checksum-verified.
